 @FLCLimaxxx  Pure speculation. Nothing you said is confirmed fact. What we know is that it's going to be around a 3060 / 3060ti. The specifics beyond that are unknown.

This is a damn good showing for a first release. Anyone who says otherwise is delusional. The fact they didn't manage to hit 3070 levels is inconsequential. Mid tier level of performance is what the vast majority of people buy, so all this does is increase their potential market base. The fact that it is favorably priced vs Nvidia is a solid win for consumers as well.

Once they get the driver's sorted, we'll have a real third option for GPUs, and that is always in the consumers benefit.

 @SpartanArmy117  Most likely because their goal was to advance on their already lucrative OEM partners, which are stuck in exclusivity agreements for CPU's and MB's from their lineup. Only to get told "No, we dont want to sign another contract until our existing 77 year contract expires. For a product that doesnt seem to be what consumers want".

Intel has never targeted consumers directly, its always been about OEM and Business contracts. It doesnt matter if it performs well or is competitive (Intel's IGP's were /never/ stable or competitive.). Contracts are just much more lucrative and provides a more stable income source then consumer-focused sales. | The fact we are seeing such a aggressive PR shift is showing that its not working this time, and they are desperately trying to offload their launch stock in a acceptable state.

Unfortunately, i cant imagine a future for intel's DGPU line, its simply not within their companies history or policies to encourage a product based around consumers. They have no incentive to do so now. So like many of their products of the past, i expect ARC DGPU's will last for as many as 2 generations, before being rebranded and rolled back to a simple IGP integration like they already are using the cores for alongside the DGPU's.

